      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Chronic, nonspecific back pain is a ubiquitous problem that has frustrated both physicians and patients. Some have suggested that it is time for a \"paradigm shift\" in treating it. One of them is John Sarno, MD, of New York University's Rusk Institute of Rehabilitation, who has argued for this in 4 books and several journal publications. We believe that a mind-body approach is more effective and involves much less risk and expense than conventional approaches in appropriately diagnosed cases."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine if a mind-body treatment program addressing a presumed psychological etiology of persistent back pain merits further research."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Case series outcome study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Single physician's office in metropolitan Los Angeles."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Fifty-one patients with chronic back pain, diagnosed with tension myositis syndrome, a diagnosis for \"functional\" back pain and treated in the principal investigator's office in 2002 and 2003."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "A program of office visits, written educational materials, a structured workbook (guided journal), educational audio CDs, and, in some cases, individual psychotherapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Pain intensity (visual analog scale scores), quality of life (RAND SF-12), medication usage, and activity level (questionnaires). Follow-up was at least 3 to 12 months after treatment."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Mean VAS scores decreased 52% for \"average\" pain (P < .0001), 35% for \"worst\" pain (P < .0001), and 65% for \"least\" pain (P < .0001). SF-12 Physical Health scores rose >9 units (P = .005). Medication usage decreased (P = .0008). Activity levels increased (P =.03). Participants aged >47 years and in pain for >3 years benefited most."
        }
      ]},
